WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.760
It's not often that I get to return back to some of the work that I've this, you know, was in 2015,

00:08.560 --> 00:17.040
was when I made this piece. The work began in 2014. And so I talked about a lot about it,

00:18.080 --> 00:24.160
you know, for a few years, but then it's been a minute since I've returned back to it. So, yeah,

00:24.160 --> 00:36.720
I appreciate the space being made for it. So this piece was made a year after our efforts as a sort of

00:37.520 --> 00:46.720
collage reflection of all of the different organizing pieces that went into We Church genocide.

00:46.880 --> 00:55.120
I think there was multiple, there were so many different stories that came out of We Church genocide.

00:55.120 --> 01:02.160
And I wanted this piece to be a portal for that storytelling and all of the different,

01:04.880 --> 01:12.320
all of the different creative actions and strategies and moments that that came out of the work.

01:13.200 --> 01:22.320
And so in this piece, you know, you see the black silhouettes of the eight of us joining hands with

01:23.120 --> 01:27.520
a few other people. I think actually this is just the eight of us that went to Geneva, Switzerland,

01:28.400 --> 01:39.200
with our report. We Church genocide that was re-uplifting the 1951 We Church genocide, original petition,

01:39.520 --> 01:49.520
that was charging the US with genocide of black people between 1945 and 1951. I think it had

01:49.520 --> 01:59.680
documented over 153 instances of lynchings of black men across the country. And what we wanted to do

01:59.760 --> 02:10.160
in Chicago was lift up genocide as not just this notion of immediate death, but of this slow

02:11.280 --> 02:21.200
progression of not having a grocery store within five miles of a black and brown community,

02:21.200 --> 02:28.560
not having mental health resources, not having, so like the slow death march of

02:29.200 --> 02:38.640
and of policing and of not, you know, resourcing black communities and brown communities. And so

02:39.360 --> 02:43.760
we wanted to lift that up in Chicago around police violence and lifting up the story of

02:45.440 --> 02:56.000
policing as inherently violent, as policing as just like tied to this legacy, this long legacy of

02:56.960 --> 03:03.520
of violence all the way to slave patrol, right? So we were trying to take all of that history and

03:03.520 --> 03:08.400
putting it into like a current context of the violence that young people experienced in Chicago.

03:08.400 --> 03:14.000
And so we took this report that included youth testimonies, it included data, it included all

03:14.000 --> 03:21.360
of these different pieces and took it to the UN. We were the youngest, we were the youngest

03:22.080 --> 03:29.200
group of people who delivered a report to the UN. We broke protocol, we, you know, we

03:29.200 --> 03:34.400
you're supposed to like sit and read your report and ethos vets van lear, who is an incredible

03:34.400 --> 03:41.360
public cultural organizer, like stood up and read their piece as like a poem, as spoken word

03:41.360 --> 03:47.280
poem instead of just like sitting and reading the the top lines of the report. You know, we held

03:47.760 --> 03:53.680
up art in the space. We held up the, so this going back to the silhouette, the silhouette is of us

03:53.680 --> 04:02.880
holding standing up when the US is responding to some of the questions of the UN around

04:04.640 --> 04:12.400
to the use of tasers. And so we, not happy with the US's response, of course, being like,

04:12.480 --> 04:18.000
oh, you know, our police are trained because they, you know, they visit our website and they read

04:18.000 --> 04:22.880
the how-to for how to use tasers. And so they're trained. And we're like, that's not, that isn't,

04:22.880 --> 04:30.960
that isn't it. And so, you know, we, we, um, stood up and held our arms up and

04:30.960 --> 04:36.960
fists up for a while for I think 30 minutes. And security was called, they didn't know what to do,

04:36.960 --> 04:44.080
it had never happened before. So it was, it was a moment. And I think that was like a really

04:44.080 --> 04:50.560
powerful moment to sort of like having the foreground of this piece. And so then in the background

04:50.560 --> 04:56.240
was like sort of all of the like historic and then like moments that led up to that moment

04:56.240 --> 05:03.520
and we church on a site. So like you have, um, in the top right corner, you have CPD killed Deimo

05:03.520 --> 05:08.880
and Reston Pisteimo and Deimo Dominique Franklin was a 23 year old black man who was

05:08.880 --> 05:17.840
tased and died due to the tazing a few days later by a police officer first thing about a

05:17.840 --> 05:28.080
lubricer from a Walgreens. And it was, uh, the, Deimo was a friend of people who went to,

05:28.080 --> 05:33.280
you know, of ethos and people who went to Geneva, friends of friends, um, and community. And so

05:33.760 --> 05:41.120
we carried his name to the UN with us and held up his, his photo and held up his, you know,

05:41.120 --> 05:45.840
and if anything, if it didn't, if it didn't change anything, we were like at least it was a form of,

05:47.440 --> 05:54.480
making space and a container for the grief that community felt, um, and, and gave folks a space to,

05:56.080 --> 05:59.040
you know, to, to share that grief and to feel that grief.

05:59.840 --> 06:04.320
Um, right below it, there was like a, during We Charge, Genocide's efforts, there was a bus ride,

06:04.800 --> 06:10.400
um, to Kalimit City, which is a suburb, um, right outside of Chicago, um, and it says justice for

06:10.400 --> 06:16.720
Stefan, um, and it was a freedom ride to, um, uh, to demand justice for the murder of Stefan Watts,

06:16.720 --> 06:22.880
who was a young black man, uh, killed by police and Kalimit City. Uh, he, um, was a black disabled,

06:23.840 --> 06:29.760
um, man who, um, yeah, was killed by the police and so we, we wanted to make a solidarity, um,

06:29.760 --> 06:35.520
ride out there to, to that area. Um, and then in the top left, you'll see like, um,

06:36.160 --> 06:41.600
Paul Robison, who was one of the original, uh, drafters of We Charge Genocide's report.

06:42.400 --> 06:47.840
There's the reparations fight that was happening in, in, you know, a parallel to We Charge Genocide.

06:47.920 --> 06:55.040
So there's just like a lot in there that I just wanted to offer to, uh, to our communities and to,

06:55.040 --> 07:02.640
and beyond to just sort of, like I said, a portal to storytelling, right, and, and also just to add

07:02.640 --> 07:08.960
historical context, um, and then inspiration and, and, and building sort of like this muscle of hope

07:08.960 --> 07:14.880
that like, this work had happened and we can build upon it and continue the work, um, moving forward.

07:14.880 --> 07:19.920
I think I leaned into facelessness because I wanted, um, and an emity for young people who I

07:19.920 --> 07:25.840
didn't have the consent up to draw and include in these pieces. Um, that was like one, but also I

07:25.840 --> 07:32.640
think it works because for me, I want people to see themselves reflected in the piece and to be

07:32.640 --> 07:40.800
able to place their face on there as they view it being, um, yeah, just like, uh, being this like

07:40.880 --> 07:51.680
space that they can, yeah, see themselves in. Um, and the mediums are our watercolor, um,

07:52.720 --> 08:02.480
and, uh, micro pens, um, ink and, then scanned digital, to digitize digitize and sort of like

08:02.480 --> 08:09.200
enhance the contrasting of the piece, um, was the way I went because sometimes the watercolors didn't

08:09.200 --> 08:14.240
work out in the, in the ways that I wanted them to. I miss them. It's like, I want more pigment.

08:14.640 --> 08:22.560
Um, so yeah, so, and then this stuff I think maybe just a couple of hours to, to create. I remember,

08:23.600 --> 08:31.360
I, I remember sketching it out. I was living actually in the Bay Area at the time, um, sort of like a small,

08:31.360 --> 08:37.280
like the only five months I ever lived outside of Chicago in my life. So I went, um, and had like an

08:37.360 --> 08:44.000
artist, uh, studio there. And it's, yeah, I just remember, you know, sketching this piece and, um,

08:44.000 --> 08:50.000
laying the color on within just a few hours and then, uh, scanning and sending over to Mariam

08:50.000 --> 08:56.160
Kaba. And she was like, I love this piece so much. Let's give it out to everyone who participated.

08:56.160 --> 09:01.600
And then she submitted it to the DuSable Museum. And she was like, it's in permanent collection now.

09:01.600 --> 09:05.040
And I was like, oh, thanks. Thanks. I appreciate that.

